# The candidate retail Cockpit, alongside the retail installation.
# Copy to .env.retail-candidate and adjust. Never committed with real values.
#
# Two lines need YOUR values before this works: DATABASE_URL and SECRET_KEY.
# Everything else below is correct as it stands.
#
# EVERY line here is `VAR=${VAR:-default}`, so THIS FILE PROVIDES DEFAULTS AND
# YOUR SHELL WINS. That is not decoration. The launcher sources this file
# under `set -a`, and when these were bare `VAR=value` assignments each one
# silently overwrote what the operator had exported -- which is exactly how a
# live UAT reached the engine with the fail-closed placeholder price card
# while the preflight, reading the shell, reported the candidate card and said
# LIVE READY. Two configurations, no comparison, one 503.
#
# Keep the `${VAR:-...}` form when you add a line.

# --- the candidate's own database -------------------------------------------
# ITS OWN. The retail API writes investigations, projects and saved analyses,
# so pointing this at the demo's database would mutate the demo -- which is
# the one thing that would stop the existing Retail Demo being separately
# recoverable. Create it once:
#
#   createdb creditprobe_retail_candidate
#   DATABASE_URL=... .venv/bin/python -m alembic upgrade head
#
DATABASE_URL=${DATABASE_URL:-postgresql+psycopg://USER@127.0.0.1:5432/creditprobe_retail_candidate}

# Any long random string. `python3 -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"`
SECRET_KEY=${SECRET_KEY:-CHANGE-ME}

# --- which application this is ----------------------------------------------
CREDITPROBE_PRODUCT_PROFILE=${CREDITPROBE_PRODUCT_PROFILE:-retail}
# Deterministic acceptance runs with no login. Set true to exercise the
# default-deny boundary instead; the Cockpit proxy answers 401 without a
# session either way, which tests/retail_cockpit/test_host.py proves.
REQUIRE_LOGIN=${REQUIRE_LOGIN:-false}

# --- the book, COPIED into the candidate ------------------------------------
# The candidate reads its own copy of the published retail book and never the
# installation's directories, so nothing it does can reach them. Populate it
# once, reading the demo and writing only here:
#
#   rsync -a /path/to/demo/data/retail/analytics/  var/retail-candidate-book/analytics/
#   rsync -a /path/to/demo/metadata/retail/        var/retail-candidate-book/metadata/
#
# About 2 GB, plus ~520 MB for the release published from it.
DATA_ANALYTICS_DIR=${DATA_ANALYTICS_DIR:-var/retail-candidate-book/analytics}
METADATA_DIR=${METADATA_DIR:-var/retail-candidate-book/metadata}

# --- which book the Cockpit reads -------------------------------------------
# The release projected from the published Cockpit Data domain. Produced by
# scripts/retail_cockpit/publish_release.py, which reads the copy above
# read-only and writes only into the Cockpit lake beside it.
COCKPIT_V4_RETAIL_RELEASE_ID=${COCKPIT_V4_RETAIL_RELEASE_ID:-cockpitdata-r1.2.0-c1.0.0-s20260910-p7}

# --- the Cockpit engine ------------------------------------------------------
COCKPIT_AGENTIC_V4=${COCKPIT_AGENTIC_V4:-true}
# Deliberately false. The pre-domain V3 lake is never written by this
# candidate, so its namespace cannot collect anything.
COCKPIT_AGENTIC_V3=${COCKPIT_AGENTIC_V3:-false}
COCKPIT_V4_API_PORT=${COCKPIT_V4_API_PORT:-8415}

# --- the candidate's own runtime --------------------------------------------
# State, artifacts, logs, pids and the session spill directory all live here.
COCKPIT_V4_RUNTIME_DIR=${COCKPIT_V4_RUNTIME_DIR:-var/retail-cockpit-candidate/runtime}

# --- how much memory one session may hold ------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY UNSET. The engine's own 1536MB is what this candidate runs on.
#
# The benchmark on a 16 GB Linux container chose 3072MB: the lowest value that
# materially reduces spilling. The demo Mac reported 3,033 MiB AVAILABLE, and
# 3072MB needs about 3,533 MiB resident -- so that choice is withdrawn here.
# The engine materialises the book at startup and holds it, so this is a
# steady-state footprint sitting beside the demo and a browser, not a spike.
#
# Raise it only on the Mac's own measurement, in demo-ready state:
#   .venv/bin/python scripts/retail_cockpit/benchmark_session.py \
#       --limits 1536MB,2048MB --repeat 3
#   available >= 3,500 MiB  -> 2048MB   (~12 s start, ~2.2 GB resident)
#   available <  3,500 MiB  -> leave it unset  (1536MB, ~18-45 s start)
# The benchmark refuses a limit the machine cannot hold rather than swapping.
#
# COCKPIT_V4_SQL_MEMORY_LIMIT=2048MB

# Where a session spills. Defaults to <COCKPIT_V4_RUNTIME_DIR>/duckdb-temp and
# is REFUSED if it resolves outside the runtime directory.
# COCKPIT_V4_SQL_TEMP_DIR=

# --- the provider ------------------------------------------------------------
# Names only. The credential is never written here; it is read from the
# environment the launcher inherits.
#
# For a DETERMINISTIC acceptance run, start the launcher with
# RETAIL_COCKPIT_OFFLINE=1 and leave the credential unset: the engine gets a
# provider that cannot reach the network, so no paid call is possible. The
# banner then says READY (OFFLINE) so nobody reads it as "it can answer".
# The analyst model. An OFFLINE run needs neither this nor a price card --
# the provider cannot reach the network, so the runtime is built from an
# offline capability that names no model and declares no price.
AI_COCKPIT_REASONING_MODEL=${AI_COCKPIT_REASONING_MODEL:-claude-opus-5}

# The credential. NEVER written here -- not because the file would clobber
# your export (it no longer would), but because a key in a file is a key that
# gets committed, copied and pasted. Export it in the shell that runs the
# launcher; the engine subprocess inherits it:
#
#   export COCKPIT_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...        # in your shell, not here
#   launchers/retail/start-retail-candidate.command
#
# COCKPIT_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The price card for that model, with all four billing classes.
#
# NOT config/cockpit_v4/price_card.json: that is the shipped placeholder
# whose only key is REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-MODEL-ID, it fails closed for every
# real model by design, and it is part of the verbatim port. The candidate
# card beside it carries Anthropic's published first-party rates for
# claude-opus-5, transcribed with their source and date.
#
# If you bill through Bedrock or Google Cloud, hold a negotiated discount, or
# pin inference_geo='us' (a 1.1x multiplier on all four classes), those rates
# are WRONG for you and the card needs your own.
#
# A zero price is accepted by the loader and silently disables the per-run
# spend ceiling, so never leave one in a live card.
COCKPIT_V4_PRICE_CARD=${COCKPIT_V4_PRICE_CARD:-config/cockpit_v4/price_card.candidate.json}

# --- the cumulative spend cap -----------------------------------------------
# The engine bounds ONE run (spend_ceiling_usd, 1.50 in standard mode) and
# counts nothing across runs, so twelve runs cost twelve times that. This is
# the cumulative half, enforced by the retail proxy before a run reaches the
# engine, and read from the engine's own ledger.
#
# Unset, there is no cap at all. LIVE_UAT_PLAN.md budgets USD 15.00.
RETAIL_COCKPIT_SPEND_CAP_USD=${RETAIL_COCKPIT_SPEND_CAP_USD:-15.00}

# --- a fresh ledger for the UAT ----------------------------------------------
# The cap counts everything in the engine's state store, INCLUDING runs from
# before the cap existed. Pointing the store at a new file starts a clean
# ledger, so the $15 budgets the twelve questions and nothing else. Nothing is
# deleted: the existing store stays where it is, as evidence.
#
# Uncomment for the UAT, and leave it set for its resumed runs so `--from N`
# counts against the same budget.
#
# COCKPIT_V4_STATE_DATABASE=var/retail-cockpit-candidate/runtime/state/uat.sqlite3

# --- how the browser reaches the Cockpit -------------------------------------
# `same-origin` puts the Cockpit behind the retail API rather than on the
# engine's own port: the client turns every call into a relative
# /api/v1/cockpit-v4/... against the page's origin. That is what lets the
# session cookie travel (the client's EventSource sets no withCredentials, so
# a cross-origin stream would carry none) and what keeps the app's default-deny
# middleware in front of every Cockpit call.
#
# It also means Next's own /api/* rewrite must reach the retail API. It
# defaults to port 8000; unset, every Cockpit call answers 500 while the API
# is healthy.
NEXT_PUBLIC_COCKPIT_V4_API=${NEXT_PUBLIC_COCKPIT_V4_API:-same-origin}
BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL=${BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:8329}

# --- the boundary between the proxy and the engine ---------------------------
# Generated per launch by the candidate launcher and given to both processes.
# A default would be the same on every machine, so there is none.
# RETAIL_COCKPIT_HOST_SECRET=
RETAIL_COCKPIT_ENGINE_URL=${RETAIL_COCKPIT_ENGINE_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:8415}
RETAIL_COCKPIT_TENANT=${RETAIL_COCKPIT_TENANT:-demo-tenant}
